391], ["CC", -8.264320373535156]]}, {"t": " split", "lp": -0.5956085324287415, "top": [[" split", -0.5956085324287415], [" distinction", -0.837218165397644]]}, {"t": ".", "lp": -0.2902012765407562, "top": [[".", -0.2902012765407562], [" (", -1.5426007509231567]]}, {"t": " It", "lp": -0.8854151368141174, "top": [[" It", -0.8854151368141174], [" H", -1.6354780197143555]]}, {"t": "'s", "lp": -0.9364923238754272, "top": [["'s", -0.9364923238754272], [" gives", -1.0764553546905518]]}, {"t": " the", "lp": -0.08041871339082718, "top": [[" the", -0.08041871339082718], [" more", -2.8658783435821533]]}], "text": "# CCS Mode on Intel Arc (xe driver)\n\nThe `ccs_mode` parameter controls how **Compute Compute Subsystems (CCS)** are exposed to the driver. This is primarily relevant for **CUDA/ROCm-style GPU compute** and some machine learning workloads that need to distinguish between \"small\" and \"large\" cores.\n\n## What CCS Are\n\nYour Arc A770 has two classes of cores:\n- **CCS (small cores)** \u2013 lightweight cores for background/compute tasks\n- **GCS (large cores)** \u2013 the main graphics/compute cores\n\nThe driver can present these to user space in different ways.\n\n## The Modes\n\n**`ccs_mode=1`**\n- Exposes CCS cores **separately** as their own device instances\n- The small cores are visible as distinct from the large cores\n- Useful when you need fine-grained control over which cores execute work\n\n**`ccs_mode=2`**\n- CCS cores are **hidden** from user space\n- Only the large (GCS) cores are presented as the compute device\n- This is often the **safer default** for workloads that don't understand the mixed-architecture design\n\n**`ccs_mode=4`**\n- This is a **higher bitmask** combining flags. The CCS mode field is actually a bitmask, so `4` typically means *\"use mode 1 but with additional behavior\"* \u2014 specifically it often enables CCS exposure **plus** some extended feature flag.\n\n## When to Use Each\n\n- **Default / general use**: You usually don't need to set this at all. Let the driver decide.\n- **ccs_mode=2**: Use this if you encounter **instability or crashes** with compute workloads, or if your application doesn't understand the CCS/GCS split. It's the"}